<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Questions"><div class="qtable"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">#</th><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="num">Q1</td><td class="q"><a href="#q1">How far does the analogy of classic TDD with greedy pathing or gradient descent hold, and do variants that change the map, the step size, the breadth of search or the check reach a better end point?</a></td><td class="st"><span class="chip" data-k="good"><i></i>answered</span></td></tr><tr><td class="num">Q2</td><td class="q"><a href="#q2">Can each TDD methodology be written as a short numbered procedure that an AI coding agent carries out unattended from start to finish?</a></td><td class="st"><span class="chip" data-k="good"><i></i>answered</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
